---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { href } from "../lib";
---

<Base
  title="Secure delivery setup"
  description="Hardened, reusable pipelines, protection rules and short-lived cloud access, set up in your own GitHub organisation and handed over for your team to own."
>
  <p class="eyebrow">Package 2</p>
  <h1>Secure delivery setup</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Pipelines that are safe by default and easy for every team to use, set up in your repositories and
    handed over. You own all of it, with no licence and no support contract.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <ul class="ticks">
    <li>Teams on GitHub whose pipelines have grown repo by repo, each a little different.</li>
    <li>Teams that hold long-lived cloud keys in CI and want rid of them.</li>
    <li>Teams that need to show customers or auditors how their software is built and released.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What is included</h2>
  <ul class="ticks">
    <li>Shared, reusable workflows for build, test, security scanning and deployment, which your repositories call instead of copying.</li>
    <li>Protection rules for your main branches and releases, defined as code, with a check that reports if anyone weakens them.</li>
    <li>Automatic, reviewed updates for dependencies and pipeline components.</li>
    <li>Short-lived access from pipelines to AWS, scoped to the repository and environment, replacing stored keys.</li>
    <li>Signed builds, so you can show exactly where a release came from, where you need it.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Every change arrives as a pull request in your repositories, so your team reviews each piece as it
    lands. <a href={href("how-we-build/")}>See how we run the same setup ourselves</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What you receive</h2>
  <ul class="ticks">
    <li>Working pipelines in your repositories, and the rules that protect them.</li>
    <li>Written documentation of how it fits together and how to change it.</li>
    <li>A handover session with your engineers, who will already have used and changed it during the work.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What is not included</h2>
  <ul class="ticks">
    <li>Running or supporting the pipelines afterwards. Your team owns them from the handover.</li>
    <li>Changes to your application code.</li>
    <li>Building new cloud environments or Kubernetes clusters.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If you use our public workflows, later improvements reach you as versioned releases, offered as
    pull requests that your team chooses whether to merge.
  </p>

  <p class="cta"><a class="button" href={href("contact/")}>Discuss a setup</a></p>
</Base>
